Block Subsidy
- The number of new bitcoins generated as a rewards for successful bitcoin minor of a block. A successful block miner is the machine which was able to solve the new block mining challange. 

This probably makes no sense if you have little to no knowledge of how the bitcoin mining process work.

Lets take a step back to explain bitcoin mining
- The process begins when a new transaction is created by a Bitcoin (with capital 'B' refers to the system whereas with the lowercase 'b' refers to the cryptocurrency) user
- A transaction is just the transfer bitcoins from one account to another (not really a transfer but more like a change of ownership within the system) 
- When a new transaction is created within the system it is then broadcasted into the bitcoin network (a network of nodes called miners which all have a copy of the blockchain)
- Once all the miners get the copy of the transaction, which lends in a memory buffer called memory pool, the miner gets a bunch of transactions from the memory pool to make a candidate "block" into the blockchain. This is done by all the miners. 
- The miners once the have generated a "block" (a group of transaction grouped together into a block and some block header data, metadata) they then compete to solve a problem generated by the system 
- The miner that win's the challenge (solves the problem first) get to be the one with the winning block which and is allowed by the system to continue to add the block into the blockchain and broadcast the block into the blockchain so all other miners can sync their copy of the blockchain 
- Note: the mining process is the only process by which new bitcoin are created and introduced into the system 
- Where do the new bitcoins go?
- New bitcoins are awarded to the successful miner as an incentive 

What is the incentive for miners?
- Miners are only incentivised for every successfully mining (first one to solve a candidate block challange)
- Remember a block is a group of transaction grouped together and some block header data (block metadata). Each of the transaction include a transaction fee - some amount of bitcoins included in the transaction as an offer to the network miners to prioritise them in the inclusion to the next candidate block)
- Miner's total rewards = the sum of all the transaction fees from each of the transaction included into the successful block to be included into the block chain + the block subsidy which is determined by the height of the new block in the block chain 
- The miner's reward is also actioned through a new transaction which is addressed to the successful miner's address 
- This transaction is called the coinbase and is added into the block before it is added into the blockchain 

How is the block subsidy determined?
- The block subsidy (amount of new bitcoin generated in the system and awarded to the successful miner) is a function of the hight of the successful block which is to be added into the blockchain and the initial block subsidy (decided an set by Satoshi)
- Interesting fact: there are only 21 million bitcoins which can be generated into the bitcoing network (total bitcoin supply). The total bitcoin supply is constrained by the initial bitcoin subsidy set by Satoshi and the process of halving of the bitcoin subsidy at every 210 000 blocks into the bitcoin (which is estimated to be every four years).
